A developer builds the matcher from a shared list:

```ts
import { PROTECTED } from './routes';

export const config = {
  matcher: PROTECTED.map((p) => `${p}/:path*`),
};
```

Why doesn't this behave like listing the same strings inline?

Proxy should pass a tenant ID to the page and route handlers without exposing it to the browser. Which code does that?

```ts
const h = new Headers(request.headers);
h.set('x-tenant', tenantId);
```

A Pages Router app uses this matcher and wants Proxy to skip data requests:

```ts
export const config = {
  matcher: '/((?!api|_next/data|_next/static|_next/image).*)',
};
```

What happens for requests to `/_next/data/<build-id>/account.json`?

Signed-out users report "too many redirects". What's wrong?

```ts
export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  if (!request.cookies.has('session')) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url));
  }
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)',
};
```

To skip expensive work for router prefetches, a developer writes this in `proxy.ts`, but the branch never runs:

```ts
if (request.headers.get('next-router-prefetch')) {
  return NextResponse.next();
}
```

Why, and what does the docs suggest instead?

After this Proxy change, form submissions that call Server Actions start failing and some streamed pages break. What is the cause?

```ts
export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const headers = new Headers(request.headers);
  headers.set('x-user', getUserFromCookie(request));
  return NextResponse.next({ headers });
}
```

Proxy sends an analytics event per request. Version 1 awaits it before responding; version 2 uses `event.waitUntil()`:

```ts
// v1
await fetch(analyticsUrl, { method: 'POST', body });
return NextResponse.next();

// v2
event.waitUntil(fetch(analyticsUrl, { method: 'POST', body }));
return NextResponse.next();
```

What is the practical difference?

A Proxy file guards interview sessions with this matcher entry:

```ts
export const config = {
  matcher: ['/dashboard/:path*', '/interviews/:path+'],
};
```

For which pair of requests does Proxy run?

To rate-limit logins, a developer keeps a counter in Proxy:

```ts
const hits = new Map<string, number>();

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const key = request.headers.get('x-forwarded-for') ?? 'anon';
  hits.set(key, (hits.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);
  if (hits.get(key)! > 20) return new Response('Too many', { status: 429 });
}
```

It passes local tests but doesn't limit anything in production. What is the best explanation?
